+
+# Decision: Whole-visit revisions for concurrent visit edits
+
+## Context
+
+The record store defines what a current visit means when independently edited copies arrive. Field workers on 12 shared tablets can be disconnected for 48 hours, and workers sometimes correct the same visit independently after comparing field notes. About 6,000 plot-visit records per season feed a reviewed CSV that a regional science partner receives every Friday, with two coordinators clearing the review queue.
+
+The mechanism chosen here determines how much review work a pair of conflicting edits costs, whether an exported value is a whole submitted visit or a stitched one, and how much standing logic the team must maintain. It is also the contract tablet-sync and review-export are sized around. Both candidate mechanisms respect the standing constraint that no submitted edit may be discarded; the choice is not driven by that constraint.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+**Gates**
+
+- **G1 — No silent combination of scientifically related fields.** A visit's scientific fields carry relationships a server cannot infer from field names alone: a species re-identification can make its cover estimate stale, an observer correction can accompany a cover re-estimate. A mechanism may not combine edits to such fields without a human seeing the combination. (Doc-local gate.)
+
+**Weighted criteria**
+
+- **C1 — Review workload per conflicting visit** (major): how much coordinator attention a pair of same-base edits costs before the Friday export.
+- **C2 — Inspectability from submission to exported value** (major; tenet: *keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable*).
+- **C3 — Operability by two coordinators without a systems administrator** (moderate; tenet: *prefer workflows two coordinators can operate without a dedicated systems administrator*).
+- **C4 — Offline submission simplicity** (moderate; tenet: *let field workers continue collecting observations without connectivity*): how much state a tablet must keep correct across a multi-day disconnection.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Whole-visit revisions
+
+**Description.** Each edit is a complete copy of the visit naming the base revision it was made against. The server stores every submission immutably; same-base concurrency produces competing complete revisions; a coordinator resolves by selecting one revision to carry forward. Nothing is ever combined.
+
+**Demonstration.** Two workers both fetch revision 4 of a visit. One corrects an observer name; the other adds a missing species. The first submission becomes revision 5 and is current; the second competes against it. A coordinator compares the two complete visits and selects one; the other stays in history.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- G1: **passes** — no combination exists to hide a relationship violation in.
+- C1: **weak** — every same-base pair is review work, even when the edits touch unrelated fields.
+- C2: **strong** — an exported value is one whole submitted visit, selected by name; provenance is the revision chain itself.
+- C3: **strong** — no rules to maintain; the recurring work is the review the team already plans for.
+- C4: **strong** — the tablet resubmits one complete payload and tracks a single base revision ID.
+
+**Summary.** Trades coordinator attention for a mechanism the team can trust and operate.
+
+### Field-aware three-way merge
+
+**Description.** Each edit carries its base snapshot plus a structured set of field changes. The server combines changes to disjoint fields automatically under maintained per-field merge rules (scalars take the later value, observer lists union, species entries insert or update), and routes only overlapping fields to review.
+
+**Demonstration.** The same two workers fetch revision 4 of a visit; one changes observers, the other adds a species. The server sees disjoint fields, combines both edits automatically into revision 5, and review sees nothing.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- G1: **eliminated** — automatic combination runs on field names, and the server cannot recognize every scientific relationship from them; a rule can combine a species re-identification with an unremarked cover estimate without anyone seeing it.
+- C1: **strong** — disjoint edits resolve with no coordinator time.
+- C2: **partial** — an exported value can be stitched from two submissions; per-field provenance must be designed, maintained, and shown.
+- C3: **weak** — merge rules per field type are standing logic that must evolve with the visit schema, and a wrong rule fails silently.
+- C4: **weak** — tablets hold base snapshots and structured change sets, more local state to keep correct across 48-hour disconnections.
+
+**Summary.** Buys coordinator time by putting maintained rules between two workers' observations.
+
+## Selection
+
+Whole-visit revisions. G1 eliminates field-aware three-way merge as specified: its central mechanism — automatic combination on field names — is exactly the silent combination the visit data cannot support. Among the remaining trade, whole-visit wins C2, C3, and C4; C1 is accepted deliberately — a bigger review queue over review rules that can combine scientifically related edits without anyone noticing. The decision turns on the inspectability and coordinator-operability tenets; the no-discard constraint holds under both options and played no part in the selection.
+
+**What the downstream work must accommodate.** Tablet-sync tracks the base revision ID of every local draft and resubmits the complete visit payload on each edit; no partial-update format exists to optimize against, and it surfaces an unresolved outcome as a pending coordinator decision on the worker's submission. Review-export draws its export set from resolved visits only, compares whole revisions side by side in a revision comparison screen, and receives resolution outcomes that name the selected revision and the competing set.
+
+**Why the other approach stays live.** The C1 cost is the whole price paid here: if same-base conflicts on unrelated fields prove common enough that two coordinators cannot clear the queue before the Friday export, the trade should be re-run. Re-opening requires more than volume — a future field-aware design must still satisfy G1, via relationship-aware combination or manual approval of every combined record, retain all original submissions, and provide field-level provenance.
